The probability of the number appearing 0, 1, and 2 times is \begin{align*}
&P(0) = \frac{3}{4}\cdot \frac{3}{4} = \frac{9}{16},\\
&P(1) = 2\cdot\frac{1}{4}\cdot \frac{3}{4} = \frac{6}{16},
\quad\text{and}\\
&P(2) = \frac{1}{4}\cdot \frac{1}{4} = \frac{1}{16},
\end{align*} respectively. So the expected return, in dollars, to the player is \begin{align*}
P(0)\cdot (-1) + P(1)\cdot (1) + P(2)\cdot (2) &= \frac{-9 + 6 +
2}{16} \\
&= \boxed{-\frac{1}{16}}.
\end{align*}

There are $2^{10} = 1024$ ways to answer the questions on the true/false test. However, we can also compute the number of ways of answering the questions on the true/false test by using combinations; there are $\binom{10}{k}$ ways to answer $k$ of the questions on the test with a false, so we have: $$\binom{10}{0} + \binom{10}{1} + \cdots + \binom{10}{9} + \binom{10}{10} = 2^{10}.$$ The desired answer is \begin{align*}
&\binom{10}{3} + \binom{10}{4} + \cdots + \binom{10}{9} + \binom{10}{10} \\
=\text{ }&2^{10} - \binom{10}{0} - \binom{10}{1} - \binom{10}{2} = 1024 - 1 - 10 - 45 \\
=\text{ }&\boxed{968}.
\end{align*}

There are $\binom{5}{2} = 10$ different pairs of marbles that can be drawn, and the expected value of the product is the average of the products of each pair.  This is  \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{10}[(1\times 2)&+(1\times 3)+(1\times 4)+(1\times 5)+{}\\
&(2\times 3)+(2\times 4)+(2\times 5)+(3\times 4)+(3\times 5)+(4\times 5)]\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad=\frac{85}{10} = \boxed{8.5}. \end{align*}

